Rast wrote:

> +10.8 AC and MR is like playing in easy mode.

If things were only that simple, or permanent.

> No matter how oblivious the player is, GDSM will
> still block a lot of hits, ameliorate others, and
> negate most magical attacks.

Yes, but...

As we've often heard, AC is immaterial if you never
let your PC be subjected to attack.

The exact problem, and especially for newbies, is
that good AC tempts the inexperienced or unwise
player to a shift to "combat wombat" mode, at which
point the monster difficulties rapidly escalate to
overcome the armor improvement.

Using "good AC" skillfully in NetHack is very
knowledge and experience intensive.

> But a light source needs a certain amount of skill
> to utilize (and it is still going to be worse than
> DSM).

Contrariwise, "using" a permanent good light source
is "turn on and forget" for magic lamps, not so for
Sunsword.

Making use of the information that good lighting
provides is certainly an acquired skill, but "good
intelligence" beats "superior armaments" has been
part of combat gospel at least since Sun Tsu wrote
"The Art of War".

There's no way "better armor" outweighs "better
information". NetHack's AC system makes the best
possible armor class still subject to attack, but
"seeing the enemy soon enough to evade the enemy"
and then indeed evading that enemy removes the
enemy's chance to attack.

